= Unit 1 · Fields and splitting fields

== Lecture 1 · Minimal polynomials and splitting constructions
#focus[Use evaluation kernels, extend embeddings when adjoining roots, and prove uniqueness of splitting fields. Prerequisite: the field-extension part of MM5020.]

*Recall and approach.* Recall the quotient description of an algebraic simple extension and the tower law.


A field extension can be studied through equations satisfied by its elements. The crucial distinction is between a polynomial with one chosen root and a field containing all of its roots. Throughout the course, fields and unqualified rings are commutative, rings have an identity, and homomorphisms preserve it.

#definition[Minimal polynomial and splitting field][For $alpha$ algebraic over $K$, the evaluation kernel in $K[x]$ is generated by a unique monic irreducible polynomial $m_(alpha,K)$. A splitting field of a nonzero $f in K[x]$ is an extension generated by all roots of $f$ in which $f$ splits into linear factors.]

The kernel statement follows by polynomial division: a remainder of smaller degree cannot vanish at $alpha$. Irreducibility follows because a field has no zero divisors. Consequently $K(alpha) iso K[x] slash (m)$ and its degree is $deg m$. A monic irreducible polynomial therefore serves as both a relation and a basis-size certificate.

#strategy[Define the extension on polynomial expressions and use the minimal-polynomial kernel to justify it.]

#theorem[Extending an embedding][Let $sigma:K -> L$ be a field embedding, let $alpha$ be algebraic over $K$, and suppose $beta$ in an extension of $L$ is a root of the coefficient-transformed polynomial $sigma(m_(alpha,K))$. There is a unique extension $K(alpha) -> L(beta)$ sending $alpha$ to $beta$.]
#proof[Send $sum_i a_i alpha^i$ to $sum_i sigma(a_i)beta^i$. The transformed minimal polynomial is irreducible over $sigma(K)$, so its evaluation kernel there is generated by that polynomial. Thus the formula descends through the quotient and is injective. Its values on $K$ and $alpha$ force uniqueness. The image is $sigma(K)(beta)$, which need not be all of $L(beta)$ unless $sigma(K)=L$.]

#theorem[Splitting fields exist and are unique over the base][Every nonzero polynomial has a finite splitting field (the base field suffices for a constant). Any two splitting fields are isomorphic by an isomorphism fixing the base field.]
#proof[For existence, adjoin a root of an irreducible factor using its quotient field, divide out a linear factor, and repeat. The process terminates after at most the original degree many root adjunctions, each finite. For uniqueness, extend the identity embedding of the base through a generating list of roots, choosing a root of the transformed minimal polynomial in the second splitting field at each step. Such a root exists because that polynomial divides the remaining transformed polynomial. The final embedding maps the full root set onto the other root set, with multiplicities respected. Since the second field is generated by those roots, the embedding is onto.]

#example[One root is not always enough][For $x^3-2$ over $QQ$, the real root $alpha$ generates a real field of degree three. A splitting field also needs a primitive cube root of unity $zeta$. The field $QQ(alpha,zeta)$ has degree six: $zeta$ is nonreal, so has degree two over the real field $QQ(alpha)$.]
#example[Embedding into a larger named target][Send $QQ(sqrt(2))$ into $RR$ by $sqrt(2)mapsto-sqrt(2)$. This is a valid embedding but is not onto $RR$; its image is the same quadratic subfield. The root-extension theorem promises an embedding into the target containing the chosen root. Surjectivity requires a separate generation argument.]

In splitting-field uniqueness, that argument succeeds because the image contains every root generating the target. In a general extension problem the target may contain unrelated elements, so the same conclusion would be unjustified. Keep the image field visible throughout the construction.

#checkpoint("1.1", [Why does a field embedding have trivial kernel?], [Its kernel is an ideal of a field and cannot be the whole field because one maps to one in a nonzero field.])
#takeaways[Always state the base field and distinguish an embedding from a surjective isomorphism onto the named target.]
