== Lecture 2 · Finite fields and Frobenius
#focus[Classify finite fields, identify their subfields, and distinguish repeated roots from irreducibility.]

*Recall and approach.* Recall finite-field multiplicative groups are cyclic and every element satisfies its field-size polynomial.


Every finite field has order $p^n$ and is a splitting field of $x^(p^n)-x$ over $FF_p$. Existence follows by taking the roots in a splitting field: Frobenius makes them closed under field operations, and the derivative $-1$ makes the $p^n$ roots distinct. Uniqueness follows from splitting-field uniqueness. We write $FF_(p^n)$ for its isomorphism class.

#theorem[Subfields of a finite field][The field $FF_(p^n)$ has a subfield of order $p^d$ exactly when $d|n$, and then that subfield is unique.]
#strategy[Use the tower law for necessity and the fixed points of a Frobenius power for existence.]
#proof[If a subfield has degree $d$ over $FF_p$, the tower law makes $d|n$. Conversely suppose $d|n$. The nonzero roots of $x^(p^d)-x$ satisfy $x^(p^d-1)=1$ in the cyclic group of order $p^n-1$. Since $p^d-1$ divides $p^n-1$, there are exactly $p^d-1$ such roots: in a cyclic group the equation $x^r=1$ has $r$ solutions when $r$ divides the group order. Together with zero, these form a field by Frobenius closure, of order $p^d$. Any subfield of that size consists of roots of the same polynomial, proving uniqueness.]

#definition[Separable polynomial][A polynomial over a field is separable if it has no repeated root in a splitting field. A polynomial has a repeated root exactly when it has a nonconstant common factor with its formal derivative.]

The derivative criterion follows by differentiating a factorization into linear factors. At a simple root $a$, the derivative equals the nonzero product of all other root differences times the leading coefficient. At a repeated root, both polynomial and derivative vanish. A common root is equivalent to a common irreducible factor over the base field.

#theorem[Irreducibles over finite fields are separable][Every irreducible polynomial over a finite field has distinct roots.]
#proof[An irreducible with a repeated root must divide its derivative, so the derivative must be zero by degree. In characteristic $p$, this means only exponents divisible by $p$ occur. Frobenius is a bijection on the finite coefficient field, so every coefficient has a $p$th root. The polynomial is therefore a $p$th power of a positive-degree polynomial, contradicting irreducibility.]

#warning[An irreducible polynomial over an infinite field of characteristic $p$ can be inseparable. In $FF_p(t)[x]$, $x^p-t$ is irreducible by Eisenstein at $t$ before passing to the fraction field, but its derivative is zero.]
#example[Subfields form a divisibility pattern][Inside $FF_(2^12)$, the subfields of sizes sixteen and sixty-four have intersection of size four: its degree divides both four and six, and the unique degree-two field lies in both. The field they generate has degree twelve, since its degree is divisible by both four and six and divides twelve.]

This reasoning needs uniqueness of subfields inside the ambient finite field, not just existence of abstract fields of those sizes. The intersection and compositum are concrete subfields of one fixed field, so the tower law can compare their degrees.

#checkpoint("1.2", [Which subfield sizes occur in a field with sixty-four elements?], [The divisors of six are one, two, three, and six, so the sizes are two, four, eight, and sixty-four.])
#takeaways[Frobenius simultaneously explains field arithmetic, subfields, and the separability of finite-field polynomials.]
